I have all the information I need. Here is the thorough report.

---

# Symbol Reference Floating Modal — Investigation Report

## Summary

There are **two surfaces** that show p5 symbol reference content with example code blocks. The user's description ("floating modal on the exercises view") matches the **`SearchOverlay`** (a real React Native `<Modal>` rendered inside the exercise screen). The "tap a p5 symbol in the code block" trigger actually routes to the **`/ref` page** (`SymbolDetail`). They render examples almost identically, but differ in one critical way: **only `/ref` strips `describe(...)` calls; the floating `SearchOverlay` does not.**

---

## 1. The floating modal component

### Primary: `SearchOverlay.tsx` (the actual floating `<Modal>`)

**Path:** `/workspaces/learn-p5/src/components/SearchOverlay.tsx`

This is a true React Native `Modal` (fade-in, transparent overlay, centered card). It is mounted inside the exercise screen:

**`/workspaces/learn-p5/src/app/learn/[course]/[id].tsx:1034-1038`
```tsx
<SearchOverlay
  visible={searchVisible}
  inline
  onClose={() => setSearchVisible(false)}
/>
```

With `inline` set, tapping a search result expands the modal in place to show `SymbolInlineDetail` (the symbol reference view with examples):

**`SearchOverlay.tsx:86-98`**
```tsx
<Modal visible={visible} transparent animationType="fade" onRequestClose={handleClose}>
  <View style={styles.overlay}>
    <Pressable style={StyleSheet.absoluteFill} onPress={handleClose} />
    <View style={[styles.card, { backgroundColor: colors.surfaceContainerHigh }, selected && inline && styles.cardExpanded]}>
      {selected && inline ? (
        <SymbolInlineDetail
          sym={selected}
          colorScheme={colorScheme}
          onBack={() => setSelected(null)}
          onClose={handleClose}
          onSymbolPress={handleSymbolPress}
        />
      ) : ( ...search list... )}
```

The inline detail body is `SymbolInlineDetail` at **`SearchOverlay.tsx:161-269`**.

Trigger: long-press the Run button for 2s (confirmed by `/workspaces/learn-p5/src/app/tips/index.tsx:16` — "Press and hold the Run button for 2 seconds to quickly search p5.js symbol references without leaving the exercise.").

### Secondary: `SymbolDetail` on the `/ref` route (destination of "tap a symbol in code")

**Path:** `/workspaces/learn-p5/src/app/ref/index.tsx` — `SymbolDetail` function at lines 41-278.

When a user taps a p5 symbol **inside the exercise WebView** (the instruction HTML, where symbols are wrapped in `<span class="symbol" data-symbol="ellipse">`), the WebView posts `{type:'openRef', symbol}`:

**`/workspaces/learn-p5/src/utils/editor/exerciseHtml.ts:1328-1332`**
```js
document.querySelectorAll('.symbol').forEach(function(el) {
  el.addEventListener('click', function() {
    postOpenRef(el.getAttribute('data-symbol'));
  });
});
```

The exercise screen handles it by pushing the `/ref` route (a slide-in screen, **not** a floating modal — see `app/_layout.tsx:43` which uses `animation: "slide_from_right"` with no `presentation: 'modal'`):

**`/workspaces/learn-p5/src/app/learn/[course]/[id].tsx:454-459`**
```tsx
case "editorTapped":
  setKeyboardVisible(true);
  break;
case "openRef":
  router.push(`/ref?symbol=${msg.symbol}`);
  break;
```

So: tap-in-code → `SymbolDetail` page; long-press Run → `SearchOverlay` floating modal. Both render example code blocks the same way (via `highlightSyntax`), but only `SymbolDetail` strips `describe()`.

---

## 2. How the modal renders example code blocks + the `describe(...)` occurrence

### Where example text comes from

Examples come from the `examples` array on each symbol entry in `src/data/reference.generated.json`, surfaced as `sym.examples` (typed `string[]`) via `P5SymbolView` in `/workspaces/learn-p5/src/data/reference.ts:37` and `:52`.

### Examples DO contain `describe(...)` calls

Inspection of the generated JSON confirms every example begins/ends with a p5.js accessibility `describe('caption');` statement (this is p5's own `describe()` for screen readers — a single statement, **not** jest-style `describe(name, () => {...})` blocks). Example from the actual JSON:

```
"function setup() {\n  createCanvas(100, 100);\n\n  background(200);\n\n  ellipse(50, 50, 80, 80);\n\n  describe('A white circle on a gray canvas.');\n}"
```

### `SearchOverlay.tsx` (floating modal) — does NOT strip `describe()`

**`/workspaces/learn-p5/src/components/SearchOverlay.tsx:214-248`**
```tsx
{sym.examples && sym.examples.length > 0 && (
  <>
    <Text style={[styles.detailSection, { color: colors.onSurface }]}>Examples</Text>
    {sym.examples.map((ex, i) => (
      <View key={i} style={{ marginBottom: 12 }}>
        {sym.norender ? (
          <View style={[styles.codeBlock, { backgroundColor: colors.surfaceDim }]}>
            <Text style={{ fontFamily: "JetBrainsMono", fontSize: 12, lineHeight: 18 }}>
              {highlightSyntax(ex, colorScheme).map((t, j) => (       // <-- raw `ex`, no stripping
                <Text key={j} style={{ color: t.color }}>{t.text}</Text>
              ))}
            </Text>
          </View>
        ) : (
          <>
            <WebView
              source={{ html: getExampleHtml(ex) }}                   // <-- raw `ex` fed to sketch too
              ...
            />
            <View style={[styles.codeBlock, { backgroundColor: colors.surfaceDim, marginTop: 8 }]}>
              <Text style={{ fontFamily: "JetBrainsMono", fontSize: 12, lineHeight: 18 }}>
                {highlightSyntax(ex, colorScheme).map((t, j) => (     // <-- raw `ex`, no stripping
                  <Text key={j} style={{ color: t.color }}>{t.text}</Text>
                ))}
              </Text>
            </View>
          </>
        )}
      </View>
    ))}
  </>
)}
```

Note: `getExampleHtml(ex)` at `SearchOverlay.tsx:230` is also called with the raw `ex` (including `describe()`). p5's `describe()` will run inside the sketch WebView — harmless, but the visible code block shows the `describe('...')` line at the bottom.

### `/ref/index.tsx` (`SymbolDetail`) — DOES strip `describe()` (the model to copy)

**`/workspaces/learn-p5/src/app/ref/index.tsx:19-29`**
```tsx
const DESCRIBE_RE = /describe\s*\(\s*(["'])((?:(?!\1).)*)\1\s*\)\s*;?\s*/g;

function extractDescribeCaption(code: string): string | null {
  DESCRIBE_RE.lastIndex = 0;
  const match = DESCRIBE_RE.exec(code);
  return match ? match[2] : null;
}

function stripDescribe(code: string): string {
  return code.replace(DESCRIBE_RE, "").trim();
}
```

Used at **`/workspaces/learn-p5/src/app/ref/index.tsx:146-183`**
```tsx
{sym.examples.map((ex, i) => {
  const caption = extractDescribeCaption(ex);
  const code = stripDescribe(ex);                      // <-- stripped
  return (
    <View key={i} style={{ marginBottom: 16 }}>
      <Text style={[styles.exampleCaption, ...]}>
        {caption || `Example ${i + 1}`}                // <-- caption reused as heading
      </Text>
      {sym.norender ? (
        <View ...><Text ...>{highlightSyntax(code, colorScheme).map(...)}</Text></View>
      ) : (
        <>
          <WebView source={{ html: getExampleHtml(ex) }} ... />   // note: still raw `ex` for the live sketch
          <View ...><Text ...>{highlightSyntax(code, colorScheme).map(...)}</Text></View>  // stripped `code`
        </>
      )}
    </View>
  );
})}
```

**This is exactly the inconsistency the user wants resolved:** the floating `SearchOverlay` shows the `describe('...')` line in the example code block, while the `/ref` page strips it and turns it into an "Example caption" header. The fix is to port `DESCRIBE_RE` / `extractDescribeCaption` / `stripDescribe` from `ref/index.tsx` into `SearchOverlay.tsx`'s `SymbolInlineDetail`.

---

## 3. Syntax highlighting in the modal's example blocks

### The modal uses a custom lightweight regex highlighter (NOT a WebView, NOT a library)

**Path:** `/workspaces/learn-p5/src/utils/referenceRender.tsx` — function `highlightSyntax` at lines 10-51.

```tsx
export function highlightSyntax(code: string, colorScheme?: "light" | "dark"): { text: string; color: string }[] {
  const theme = getEditorTheme("p5-learn", colorScheme || "dark");
  const KEYWORD_RE = /\b(function|if|else|for|while|return|let|const|var|new|this|class)\b/g;
  const P5_RE = new RegExp(`\\b(${P5_FUNCTION_NAMES.join("|")})\\b(?=\\()`, "g");
  const NUMBER_RE = /\b\d+(\.\d+)?\b/g;
  const STRING_RE = /("[^"]*"|'[^']*'|`[^`]*`)/g;
  const COMMENT_RE = /(\/\/.*)/g;
  const OP_RE = /([{}[\]();,.]|=>|[-+*/%&|^!<>=]=?)/g;

  const allMatches: { index: number; text: string; color: string }[] = [];
  const patterns: [RegExp, string][] = [
    [COMMENT_RE, theme.comment],
    [STRING_RE, theme.string],
    [KEYWORD_RE, theme.keyword],
    [NUMBER_RE, theme.number],
    [P5_RE, theme.function],
    [OP_RE, theme.operator],
  ];
  for (const [re, color] of patterns) {
    re.lastIndex = 0;
    let match;
    while ((match = re.exec(code)) !== null) {
      allMatches.push({ index: match.index, text: match[0], color });
    }
  }
  allMatches.sort((a, b) => a.index - b.index);

  const tokens: { text: string; color: string }[] = [];
  let lastEnd = 0;
  for (const m of allMatches) {
    if (m.index < lastEnd) continue;
    if (m.index > lastEnd) {
      tokens.push({ text: code.slice(lastEnd, m.index), color: theme.fg });
    }
    tokens.push({ text: m.text, color: m.color });
    lastEnd = m.index + m.text.length;
  }
  if (lastEnd < code.length) {
    tokens.push({ text: code.slice(lastEnd), color: theme.fg });
  }
  return tokens.length > 0 ? tokens : [{ text: code, color: theme.fg }];
}
```

It is a hand-rolled, first-match-wins regex tokenizer that emits `{text, color}[]`, rendered as nested `<Text>` spans. Colors come from the same `getEditorTheme("p5-learn", ...)` used by the CodeMirror editor theme (`/workspaces/learn-p5/src/utils/editor/themes.ts:226-242`), so the palette matches the editor but the engine is completely different.

This is invoked in the modal at `SearchOverlay.tsx:222` and `:240`:
```tsx
{highlightSyntax(ex, colorScheme).map((t, j) => (
  <Text key={j} style={{ color: t.color }}>{t.text}</Text>
))}
```
wrapped in a `<Text style={{ fontFamily: "JetBrainsMono", fontSize: 12, lineHeight: 18 }}>` (lines 221, 239).

### Comparison to the main code editor

The main editor is **CodeMirror 6 running inside a WebView**. The bundle entry is `/workspaces/learn-p5/src/utils/editor/bundleEntry.ts`:
```ts
import { basicSetup, EditorView } from "codemirror";
import { EditorState } from "@codemirror/state";
import { syntaxHighlighting, HighlightStyle, syntaxTree } from "@codemirror/language";
import { javascript } from "@codemirror/lang-javascript";
import { tags } from "@lezer/highlight";
```
This is bundled via esbuild into `src/utils/editor/codemirror-bundle.generated.ts` (`scripts/bundle-editor.mjs`) and injected into the exercise HTML. CodeMirror uses a real Lezer parser for JS via `@codemirror/lang-javascript`.

**So yes — there IS a non-WebView lighter highlighter in the codebase: `highlightSyntax` in `src/utils/referenceRender.tsx`.** It is the regex tokenizer used by both the floating modal and the `/ref` page for example code blocks, the `Usage` syntax box, and the symbol names. It is not a library; it is ~40 lines of custom regex code. There is also a `renderHighlightedCode` helper at `referenceRender.tsx:53-64` (not currently used by the modal — the modal calls `highlightSyntax` directly).

---

## 4. Reference data source

### Generator script

**Path:** `/workspaces/learn-p5/scripts/generate-reference.mjs`

- npm script: `gen-reference` → `node scripts/generate-reference.mjs` (package.json line 8), run on `postinstall` and `start` (package.json lines 6-7).
- Clones `https://github.com/processing/p5.js-website.git` (depth 1) into a temp dir (line 123), walks `src/content/reference/en` for `.mdx` files (line 134-154), parses MDX frontmatter with `js-yaml` (lines 23-29), and writes `src/data/reference.generated.json` (line 111).
- Falls back to `generateStub()` (line 225) with hardcoded symbols if the clone fails.

### How examples are pulled (lines 199-206)
```js
if (frontmatter.example) {
  const examples = Array.isArray(frontmatter.example)
    ? frontmatter.example
    : [frontmatter.example];
  symbol.examples = examples
    .map((ex) => (typeof ex === "string" ? ex.trim() : String(ex).trim()))
    .filter((ex) => ex.length > 0);
}
```
The `describe(...)` lines come from the upstream p5.js-website MDX `example` frontmatter — they are not added by this script. The script does NOT strip them.

### Structure of an entry (GeneratedSymbol)

TypeScript interface at `/workspaces/learn-p5/src/data/reference.ts:3-16`:
```ts
export interface GeneratedSymbol {
  name: string;
  module: string;
  submodule: string | null;
  class: string;
  itemtype: "method" | "property" | "type";
  description: string;
  syntax: string;
  params: { name: string; type: string; optional: boolean; description: string }[];
  overloads?: { params: {...}; chainable: boolean }[];
  chainable: boolean;
  isConstructor: boolean;
  examples?: string[];
}
```

The generator builds each entry at `generate-reference.mjs:185-197`:
```js
const symbol = {
  name,
  module: mod,
  submodule: frontmatter.submodule || null,
  class: cls,
  itemtype: frontmatter.itemtype || "method",
  description: stripHtml(frontmatter.description || ""),
  syntax: buildSyntax(name, params),
  params,
  overloads: overloads.length > 0 ? overloads : undefined,
  chainable: frontmatter.chainable === 1 || frontmatter.chainable === true,
  isConstructor: !!frontmatter.isConstructor,
};
```

Top-level JSON shape (ReferenceData at `reference.ts:18-26`):
```ts
interface ReferenceData {
  byName: Record<string, GeneratedSymbol>;
  byModule: Record<string, string[]>;
  byClass: Record<string, string[]>;
  classes: string[];
  keyboardFunctions: { label: string; insert: string; paramTypes: string[] }[];
  functionNames: string[];
  metadata: { generatedAt: string; p5Version: string; symbolCount: number };
}
```

**Field name note for the user's question:** the field is **`examples`** (array of strings), not `code` or `example`. Each string is a full p5 sketch snippet (typically a `function setup() { ... }` block) and includes a trailing `describe('caption');` statement. There is no separate `parameters` field on examples — parameters live at the symbol level as `params`. There is no `code` field; the closest analog is `syntax` (a one-liner like `ellipse(x, y, w, h)`).

---

## 5. Existing syntax-highlighting libraries in the codebase

**None of prism / highlight.js / refractor / react-syntax-highlighter / shiki / lowlight are present.** Only the CodeMirror 6 + Lezer stack (used inside the editor WebView) is installed.

`/workspaces/learn-p5/package.json` relevant entries:
```json
"@codemirror/autocomplete": "6.18.6",
"@codemirror/commands": "6.8.1",
"@codemirror/lang-javascript": "6.2.3",
"@codemirror/language": "6.11.0",
"@codemirror/state": "6.5.2",
